 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Account security and passkeys
+
+The sign-in and sign-up pages retain password authentication through the existing
+Times authentication service. After account creation, readers can add a passkey;
+`/devtools-times/account/settings` supports creating, listing, renaming, and
+removing passkeys, plus updating the account display name. Sensitive changes
+require identity verification with an existing passkey or the account password.
+The username remains managed by the existing authentication service.
+
+Passkeys use native WebAuthn APIs with JSON/capability polyfills, conditional
+sign-in autofill, and server-side verification through `@simplewebauthn/server`.
+Challenges expire after five minutes and are consumed atomically. Reauthentication
+requires user verification and a credential belonging to the signed-in account.
+Sessions use opaque, rotating, HttpOnly cookies; passwords are never stored locally.
+All authentication mutations enforce the same origin.
+
+Runtime configuration:
+
+- Use Node.js 22.13 or newer for the built-in SQLite driver (Node.js 24 recommended).
+- Set `AUTH_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS origin in production, for example
+  `https://example.com` (no path or trailing slash). Without this override, the
+  request's origin defines both the expected origin and relying party hostname.
+  Passkeys are tied to this hostname; changing it requires enrolling new passkeys.
+- Set `AUTH_DATABASE_PATH` to a writable SQLite file on a persistent volume.
+  The default is `.data/auth.sqlite`, ignored by Git. Back up this database; it
+  contains credential public keys, account mappings, challenges, and sessions.
+  This SQLite implementation is intended for a single application instance;
+  deployments with multiple instances need a shared transactional datastore.
+- Serve over HTTPS (or localhost for local development). Unsupported browsers
+  retain password sign-in. Existing passwords continue to provide recovery after
+  removing the last passkey.
+
+Provider names and icons are a local subset of the
+[Passkey Developer AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids).
+They are display hints only; unknown or zero AAGUIDs use a neutral fallback.
+Password-manager synchronization uses feature-detected WebAuthn Signal APIs.
